**Read:** [FiPy — conservative finite-volume modelling](../sources/FiPy-Conservative-Finite-Volume-Methods.lingua#r_5C155DKGJ5WYGV18EJ1SC41G0Q), the complete numerical approach and diffusion examples; [FiPy: Partial Differential Equations with Python](../sources/FiPy-Partial-Differential-Equations-with-Python.lingua#r_0CA1GV7T9X876WS57M2KH93SZB), the full paper; [Convecção e métodos numéricos — Maliska, UFSC](../sources/Conveccao-and-Numerical-Methods-Maliska-UFSC.lingua#r_71K7JMRA8M0N3Y8EKMZJT9NGQ7), the finite-volume course progression.

Integrate the local balance over each cell so a shared face flux enters one cell and leaves its neighbour with the opposite sign. This makes global conservation inspectable. For two half-cells with different conductivities, derive the face conductance from serial resistances rather than choosing an arbitrary mean.

Distinguish spatial truncation, time-integration error, iterative error and input/model uncertainty. An explicit update requires a stability condition and can require a stronger monotonicity condition to avoid unphysical overshoots. Implicit stability does not remove time-resolution or nonlinear-convergence requirements.

Read the complete relevant [FiPy — conservative finite-volume modelling](../sources/FiPy-Conservative-Finite-Volume-Methods.lingua#r_5C155DKGJ5WYGV18EJ1SC41G0Q) numerical method and [Convecção e métodos numéricos — Maliska, UFSC](../sources/Conveccao-and-Numerical-Methods-Maliska-UFSC.lingua#r_71K7JMRA8M0N3Y8EKMZJT9NGQ7) lectures. Derive the small system in [An inspectable conservative transient layered slab](An-inspectable-conservative-transient-layered-slab.lingua#r_6B5B7E7C3Z1APY0RYAC09P12ET), test it against a separate analytical/exact discrete result and refine both space and time. Preserve material/interface locations during refinement so the physical problem itself is not changed inadvertently.

